(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_mechanicalpropertiesoffluids=[{id:"neet-2026r-q022",pn:22,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Terminal velocity",q:"In the measurement of viscosity of liquids using terminal velocity experiment, spherical balls of same radius but having different densities are used. The variation of the terminal velocity (v) with the ratio of density of spherical ball (\u03C3) to density of the liquid (\u03C1), is best represented by:",o:["Graph 1: v rises linearly with \u03C3/\u03C1 (dotted extension to the left of \u03C3/\u03C1 = 1)","Graph 2: v rises linearly with \u03C3/\u03C1 (dotted extension to the left of \u03C3/\u03C1 = 1)","Graph 3: v is constant for \u03C3/\u03C1 \u2265 1","Graph 4: v = 0 at \u03C3/\u03C1 = 1 and rises linearly after it"],c:3,s:`Terminal velocity V_T = (2r\xB2/9\u03B7)(\u03C3 \u2212 \u03C1).
V_T = (2r\xB2/9\u03B7) \u03C1 (\u03C3/\u03C1 \u2212 1) \u2026(I).
Compare with y = mx + c \u2026(II).
Equation (I) is a straight line with a negative intercept, so v is zero at \u03C3/\u03C1 = 1 and rises linearly after that.
Note: options 1 and 2 are similar rising lines with dotted extensions; exact positions in dia may be imprecise`,fig:{w:350,h:235,alt:"Four graphs of v against sigma/rho, each with a vertical line at 1: (1) rising line through the origin, dotted before 1; (2) rising line whose dotted part starts on the axis before 1; (3) horizontal line from 1; (4) rising line starting at the axis at 1.",d:[["t",13,55,"(1)"],["l",60,95,60,17,"a"],["l",60,95,153,95,"a"],["l",100,95,100,19],["t",57,13,"v","e s"],["t",58,109,"0","m s"],["t",100,109,"1","m s"],["t",158,99,"\u03C3/\u03C1","s"],["l",60,95,100,73,"d"],["l",100,73,152,46],["t",188,55,"(2)"],["l",235,95,235,17,"a"],["l",235,95,328,95,"a"],["l",275,95,275,19],["t",232,13,"v","e s"],["t",233,109,"0","m s"],["t",275,109,"1","m s"],["t",333,99,"\u03C3/\u03C1","s"],["l",250,95,275,81,"d"],["l",275,81,327,53],["t",13,171,"(3)"],["l",60,211,60,133,"a"],["l",60,211,153,211,"a"],["l",100,211,100,135],["t",57,129,"v","e s"],["t",58,225,"0","m s"],["t",100,225,"1","m s"],["t",158,215,"\u03C3/\u03C1","s"],["l",100,178,153,178],["t",188,171,"(4)"],["l",235,211,235,133,"a"],["l",235,211,328,211,"a"],["l",275,211,275,135],["t",232,129,"v","e s"],["t",233,225,"0","m s"],["t",275,225,"1","m s"],["t",333,215,"\u03C3/\u03C1","s"],["l",275,211,328,178]]},sfig:{w:300,h:140,alt:"V_T against sigma/rho: a straight line rising to the right, dotted where below the horizontal axis, crossing the axis at sigma/rho = 1, with a negative intercept on the V_T axis.",d:[["l",58,100,58,15,"a"],["l",8,60,170,60,"a"],["t",54,12,"V_T","e s"],["t",176,64,"\u03C3/\u03C1","s"],["l",58,92,98,60,"d"],["l",98,60,150,22]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Pressure with depth",q:`A submarine is designed to withstand an absolute pressure of 100 atm. How deep can it go below the water surface?
(Consider the density of water = 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3, 1 atm = 1\xD710\u2075 Pa and gravitational acceleration g = 10 m/s\xB2)`,o:["9900 m","99 m","9000 m","990 m"],c:3,s:`P = P\u2080 + \u03C1gh.
100 \xD7 10\u2075 = 10\u2075 + 10\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 h.
10\u2077 = 10\u2075 + 10\u2074h.
10\xB3 = 10 + h.
h = 1000 \u2212 10 = 990 m.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q044",pn:44,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Bernoulli's equation and continuity",q:`Water flows in a streamline motion through a horizontal pipe of circular cross-section as shown in the figure. The pressure difference of water between P and Q is 15 Nm\u207B\xB2. The area of cross-section at P and Q are 40 cm\xB2 and 20 cm\xB2, respectively. The rate of flow of water through the pipe, in cm\xB3s\u207B\xB9, is:
[Take density of water = 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3]`,o:["200","300","400","100"],c:2,s:`By continuity: AP VP = AQ VQ.
40 VP = 20 VQ, so VQ = 2VP.
Using Bernoulli's equation: PP \u2212 PQ = (1)/(2)\u03C1(VQ\xB2 \u2212 VP\xB2).
15 = (1)/(2) \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 (4VP\xB2 \u2212 VP\xB2).
VP = \u221A(15/1500) = 0.1 m/s.
VQ = 0.2 m/s.
Rate = AP VP = 40 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 m\xB3/s.
= 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB3/s.
= 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 10\u2076 cm\xB3/s = 400 cm\xB3/s.`,fig:{w:300,h:100,alt:"A horizontal pipe, wide at the left with point P, narrowing at a constriction with point Q, and widening again to the right.",d:[["l",10,30,60,30],["p","M60 30 C90 30 100 40 130 40 C160 40 170 30 200 30"],["l",200,30,290,30],["l",10,70,60,70],["p","M60 70 C90 70 100 60 130 60 C160 60 170 70 200 70"],["l",200,70,290,70],["l",10,50,290,50],["t",30,22,"P","s"],["t",125,34,"Q","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q001",pn:1,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Surface tension, pressure excess",q:`Consider a water tank shown in the figure. It has one wall at x = L and can be taken to be very wide in the z direction. When filled with a liquid of surface tension S and density \u03C1, the liquid surface makes angle \u03B8\u2080 (\u03B8\u2080 << 1) with the x-axis at x = L. If y(x) is the height of the surface then the equation for y(x) is:
(take \u03B8(x) = sin\u03B8(x) = tan\u03B8(x) = dy/dx, g is the acceleration due to gravity)`,o:["d\xB2y/dx\xB2 = (\u03C1g/S)x","d\xB2y/dx\xB2 = (\u03C1g/S)y","d\xB2y/dx\xB2 = \u221A(\u03C1g/S)","dy/dx = \u221A(\u03C1g/S)x"],c:1,s:`Let A be a point on the surface at distance x with height y.
ROC is the radius of curvature at A.
Curvature = 1/ROC = |d\xB2y/dx\xB2| / (1 + (dy/dx)\xB2)^(3/2) = |d\xB2y/dx\xB2| / (1+0)^(3/2) = d\xB2y/dx\xB2, because dy/dx = tan\u03B8 \u2248 0.
Pressure difference \u0394P = S \xD7 curvature.
\u21D2 \u03C1gy = S d\xB2y/dx\xB2
\u2234 d\xB2y/dx\xB2 = \u03C1gy/S`,fig:{w:200,h:135,alt:"Axes y (up) and x (right); a wall at x = L; the liquid surface curves up from flat at the left and meets the wall at angle theta0 to a dotted horizontal.",d:[["l",39.1,122.8,39.1,3.1,""],["l",39.1,136.4,39.1,3.1,"a"],["l",4.3,122.8,187.2,122.8,"a"],["l",147.6,3.1,147.6,136.4,""],["p","M39.1 70.1 C93 70.1 130.2 57 147.6 41.5 L179.2 18",""],["l",147.6,41.5,193.4,42.8,"d"],["p","M166.2 41.5 A18.6 18.6 0 0 0 161.8 31.6",""],["t",169.9,40.3,"\u03B8\u2080","s"],["t",26.7,8.1,"y",""],["t",192,126.5,"x",""],["t",146.9,132.7,"x = L","m"]]},sfig:{w:200,h:135,alt:"Same graph with a point A on the surface at position x, its height y marked, the wall at x = L and the angle theta0.",d:[["l",37.2,122.8,37.2,4.3,"a"],["l",3.1,122.8,184.1,122.8,"a"],["l",145.1,3.1,145.1,122.8,""],["p","M37.2 70.1 C74.4 70.1 117.8 58.9 145.1 44.6 L175.5 19.8",""],["l",145.1,44.6,191,44.6,"d"],["p","M163.1 44.6 A18.6 18.6 0 0 0 158.7 34.7",""],["t",167.4,42.2,"\u03B8\u2080","s"],["c",89.3,67.6,1.6,"F"],["l",89.3,67.6,89.3,122.8,"d"],["t",89.3,56,"A","m"],["t",95.5,75.6,"y",""],["t",89.3,133.9,"x","m"],["t",143.8,133.9,"x = L","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q028",pn:28,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Surface tension",q:"A thin flat circular disc of radius 4.5 cm is placed gently over the surface of water. If surface tension of water is 0.07 Nm\u207B\xB9, then the excess force required to take it away from the surface is :",o:["1.98 mN","99 N","19.8 mN","198 N"],c:2,s:`Surface tension acts along the whole circumference of the disc (2\u03C0R).
Excess force = T \xD7 2\u03C0R.
= (7/100) \xD7 2 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 (4.5/100)
= 197.82 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N
= 19.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 N = 19.8 mN.`,sfig:{w:160,h:90,alt:"A flat circular disc on the water surface with surface tension T acting upward along its edge.",d:[["p","M28 62 A52 14 0 1 0 132 62 A52 14 0 1 0 28 62 Z","f"],["l",36,69.5,43,52.2],["l",43,71.8,50,50.6],["l",50,73.4,57,49.4],["l",57,74.6,64,48.7],["l",64,75.3,71,48.2],["l",71,75.8,78,48],["l",78,76,85,48.1],["l",85,75.9,92,48.4],["l",92,75.6,99,49],["l",99,75,106,49.9],["l",106,74.1,113,51.2],["l",113,72.8,120,53.1],["l",120,70.9,127,56],["l",30,60,30,22,"a"],["l",130,60,130,22,"a"],["l",86,70,86,48,"a"],["t",140,28,"T"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q006",pn:6,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Surface energy of soap bubble",q:"The amount of energy required to form a soap bubble of radius 2 cm from a soap solution is nearly (surface tension of soap solution = 0.03 N m\u207B\xB9)",o:["50.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J","30.16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J","5.06 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J","3.01 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J"],c:3,s:`A soap bubble has two surfaces, so energy = S \xD7 \u0394A \xD7 2.
\u0394A = 4\u03C0r\xB2 = 4 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 (2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2)\xB2 = 4 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2.
Energy = [0.03 \xD7 4 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074] \xD7 2 = 3.015 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J.`},{id:"neet-2023-q028",pn:28,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Bernoulli's principle applications",q:"The venturi-meter works on:",o:["The principle of perpendicular axes","Huygen's principle","Bernoulli's principle","The principle of parallel axes"],c:2,s:"A venturi-meter works on Bernoulli's principle."},{id:"neet-2022-q001",pn:1,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Excess pressure in soap bubble",q:"If a soap bubble expands, the pressure inside the bubble",o:["Increases","Remains the same","Is equal to the atmospheric pressure","Decreases"],c:3,s:`Excess pressure in a soap bubble is 4T/R.
So P_in = P_out + 4T/R.
When the bubble expands, R increases, so 4T/R falls.
Hence the pressure inside decreases.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q005",pn:5,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Pressure in fluids",q:"Two copper vessels A and B have the same. base area but of different shapes. A take twice the volume of water as that B requires to fill upto a particular common height. Then the correct statement among the following is:",o:["Vessel B weighs twice that of A.","Pressure on the base area of vessels A and B is same.","Pressure on the base area of A and B is not same.","Both vessels A and B weight the same."],c:1,s:`Both vessels are filled to the same height.
Pressure at the bottom is P = h\u03C1g, which depends only on height, so it is the same for both.`},{id:"neet-2022-q007",pn:7,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Terminal velocity",q:"A spherical ball is dropped in a long column of a highly viscous liquid. The curve in the graph shown, which represents the speed of the ball (v) as a function of time (t) is",o:["B","C","D","A"],c:0,s:`The ball starts with zero speed.
Its speed rises and finally becomes constant at the terminal speed.
This is the curve B.`,fig:{w:222,h:200,alt:"Speed against time with four curves A, B, C, D; B rises from the origin and flattens to a constant value.",d:[["l",30,165,30,2,"a"],["l",30,165,206,165,"a"],["t",20,12,"v","e"],["t",206,182,"t","m"],["l",30,165,170.4,23.69999999999999],["p","M30 165C30.9 156 32.4 126 35.4 111C38.4 96 41.7 84.5 48 75C54.3 65.6 63 59.1 73.2 54.3C83.4 49.5 96.5 47.7 109.2 46.2C122 44.7 133.5 45.2 149.7 45.3C165.9 45.4 197 46.8 206.4 47.1"],["p","M62.4 7.5C62.7 13.5 62.1 31.5 64.2 43.5C66.3 55.5 69 68.3 75 79.5C81 90.8 91.5 102.8 100.2 111C108.9 119.3 117.9 125 127.2 129C136.5 133.1 151.2 134.3 156 135.3"],["p","M82.2 7.5C88.2 9 107 10.8 118.2 16.5C129.5 22.2 141.3 31.2 149.7 41.7C158.1 52.2 164.5 66.5 168.6 79.5C172.7 92.6 173.1 113.3 174 120"],["t",35.4,72.3,"B"],["t",37.2,36.29999999999998,"C"],["t",181.20000000000002,110.1,"D"],["t",53.400000000000006,160.5,"A"]]},sfig:{w:170,h:170,alt:"Speed against time: curve B rises from the origin and levels off at the dashed terminal speed vT.",d:[["l",30,150,30,10,"a"],["l",10,150,150,150,"a"],["l",30,150,30,160],["t",24,16,"v","e"],["t",154,154,"t"],["l",30,60,100,60,"d"],["t",16,64,"v","e"],["t",17,68,"T","s"],["t",112,50,"B"],["p","M30 150C31.3 143.7 34.3 123.3 38 112C41.7 100.7 46.3 89.7 52 82C57.7 74.3 64 69.7 72 66C80 62.3 92 61.2 100 60C108 58.8 116.7 59.2 120 59"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q035",pn:35,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Stokes law viscous drag",q:"At the terminal velocity of a copper ball of radius 5 mm falling through a tank of oil at room temperature is 10 cm s\u207B\xB9. If the viscosity of oil at room temperature is 0.9 kg m\u207B\xB9s\u207B\xB9, the viscous drag force is:",o:["4.23 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N","8.48 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 N","8.48 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N","4.23 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 N"],c:1,s:`r = 5 mm = 5\xD710\u207B\xB3 m and v_t = 10 cm/s = 10\xD710\u207B\xB2 m/s.
Stokes' drag F = 6\u03C0\u03B7rv_t.
F = 6 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 0.9 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10\xD710\u207B\xB2 \u2248 84.78\xD710\u207B\u2074 N = 8.478\xD710\u207B\xB3 N.`},{id:"neet-2021-q026",pn:26,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Terminal velocity",q:"The velocity of a small ball of mass M and density d, when dropped in a container filled with glycerine becomes constant after some time. If the density of glycerine is d/2, then the viscous force acting on the ball will be",o:["2Mg","Mg/2","Mg","(3)/(2)Mg"],c:1,s:`Constant velocity means the net force is zero.
Forces: weight Mg down, buoyancy FB up, viscous force Fv up.
Mg = FB + Fv.
Volume V = M/d. Buoyancy = (d/2)Vg = (1)/(2)(dV)g = Mg/2.
Fv = Mg \u2013 Mg/2 = Mg/2.`,sfig:{w:240,h:135,alt:"Ball moving down at constant velocity: weight Mg down, buoyant force FB and viscous force Fv up",d:[["c",90,60,28],["l",90,60,90,118,"a"],["l",90,60,90,12,"a"],["l",57,52,57,22,"a"],["t",90,130,"Mg","ms"],["t",96,8,"F"],["t",103,11,"B","s"],["t",50,15,"F"],["t",57,18,"v","s"],["l",150,52,150,76,"a"],["t",158,68,"v = constant","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2020-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Capillary rise",q:"A capillary tube of radius r is immersed in water and water rises in it to a height h. The mass of the water in the capillary is 5g. Another capillary tube of radius 2r is immersed in water. The mass of water that will rise in this tube is :",o:["5.0 g","10.0 g","20.0 g","2.5 g"],c:1,s:`Height h = 2T cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g), so h \u221D 1/r.
Mass m = \u03C0r\xB2h\u03C1 \u221D r\xB2 \xD7 (1/r) = r.
m\u2082/m\u2081 = r\u2082/r\u2081 = 2r/r = 2.
m\u2082 = 2 \xD7 5 = 10 g.`},{id:"neet-2019-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Torricelli's law",q:"A small hole of area of cross-section 2 mm\xB2 is present near the bottom of a fully filled open tank of height 2 m. Taking g = 10 m/s\xB2, the rate of flow of water through the open hole would be nearly :",o:["2.23 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s","6.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s","12.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s","8.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s"],c:2,s:`Speed of efflux v = \u221A(2gh) = \u221A(2\xD710\xD72) = \u221A40 \u2248 6.32 m/s.
Rate of flow = A v = 2\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 6.32.
\u2248 12.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s.`},{id:"neet-2019-q117",pn:117,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Surface tension",q:"A soap bubble, having radius of 1 mm, is blown from a detergent solution having a surface tension of 2.5\xD710\u207B\xB2 N/m. The pressure inside the bubble equals at a point Z\u2080 below the free surface of water in a container. Taking g = 10 m/s\xB2, density of water = 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3, the value of Z\u2080 is :",o:["1 cm","0.5 cm","100 cm","10 cm"],c:0,s:`Excess pressure inside a soap bubble (two surfaces) = 4T/R.
= 4 \xD7 2.5\xD710\u207B\xB2 / 10\u207B\xB3 = 100 Pa.
This equals \u03C1gZ\u2080 = 10\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 Z\u2080 = 10\u2074 Z\u2080.
Z\u2080 = 10\u207B\xB2 m = 1 cm.`}]})();
